// DEATH-RECALL TAG: gunshot aftermath, phrased via the "kills NAME" cue.
// Expected: HIT. Also contains a post-death FLASHBACK scene (Marcus alive)
// to test the extractor's non-literal-scene exclusion guard.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - LIVING ROOM - NIGHT

A lamp knocked sideways throws long shadows across a room mid-argument. PRIYA OYELARAN, 20s, stands with her phone already dialing. Her older brother MARCUS OYELARAN, 30s, takes it out of her hand and ends the call.

MARCUS
Not yet. If it's nothing, a squad car in the driveway makes us the story on every porch camera on the block by morning.

PRIYA
That thing downstairs is not a raccoon, Marcus. You always do this. You decide first and ask me after.

MARCUS
I decide first because I'm the one who has to explain it to Mom if I'm wrong. Get in your room. Lock it.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - HALLWAY - CONTINUOUS

Marcus pulls a bat from behind the hall closet door. Priya lingers at her doorway, unconvinced, watching him check the stairwell.

PRIYA
At least take my phone. If something happens I want a way to know.

MARCUS
Nothing is happening. Lock the door, Priya. I mean it this time.

She goes in. The latch clicks. Marcus starts down the stairs one at a time, bat low.

EXT. OYELARAN HOUSE - BACK PORCH - NIGHT

The kitchen door's window is broken from the outside in, glass scattered on the mat. A gloved hand reaches through and turns the deadbolt.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT

DEV KASPRZAK, 30s, jacket zipped to the chin, is already inside when Marcus rounds the corner. Neither man expected the other to be this close.

MARCUS
You're in the wrong house. Whatever Ellis told you I owe him, it isn't here.

DEV
Ellis didn't send me for money, Marcus. He sent me for the ledger you've been holding over him since March.

MARCUS
There's no ledger. Get out before I make you leave the way you came in.

Dev's hand comes out of his jacket holding a pistol, low and unsteady.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT (CONTINUOUS)

Marcus raises the bat a half-second too late. The shot catches him in the shoulder and he staggers into the counter, refusing to go down, reaching for the drawer behind him.

DEV
Don't. Don't make me do this again.

MARCUS
Priya, stay upstairs—

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - NIGHT (SECONDS LATER)

The second shot kills Marcus before he can move another step. Dev stands over him a moment, then bolts through the broken door into the dark.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - BEDROOM - NIGHT

Priya, phone pressed to her ear, hears the second shot through the floor and goes still.

PRIYA
(to dispatcher)
Two shots. My brother's downstairs. Please, someone needs to come now.

She's already moving for the door despite everything he told her.

EXT. OYELARAN HOUSE - NIGHT

Red and blue light washes the front lawn. Priya sits on the porch step wrapped in a paramedic's blanket, staring at nothing while officers move in and out of the house behind her.

INT. POLICE STATION - INTERVIEW ROOM - NIGHT

Fluorescent light, a scratched table. DETECTIVE OKAFOR, 50s, slides a legal pad toward Priya, who hasn't touched the coffee in front of her.

OKAFOR
Take your time. I know how tonight went. I just need what you remember, in order, as close to exact as you can get it.

PRIYA
He said "not yet" and "get in your room" and then he went down the stairs by himself, because that's what he always does. That's the order.

OKAFOR
Did your brother know somebody named Ellis? Or a Kasprzak?

PRIYA
Ellis owed him money for a job neither of them will admit was under the table. I don't know a Kasprzak.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY (FLASHBACK)

Sunlight instead of shadow. A YOUNGER PRIYA, twelve, stands on a step stool at the counter while MARCUS, twenties, guides her hands over a mixing bowl.

MARCUS
Fold it, don't stir it. Stirring knocks the air back out — you'll spend an hour getting it back in.

YOUNGER PRIYA
You're just making that up so I do it your way.

MARCUS
Maybe. Try it my way once and tell me I'm wrong.

He steals a fingerful of batter off the spoon before she can stop him. She swats his arm, laughing.

INT. POLICE STATION - HALLWAY - DAY

Okafor meets Priya outside the interview room, folder under his arm.

OKAFOR
Ellis rolled on him inside an hour. Kasprzak did three debt collections for Ellis this year alone, all off the books. This wasn't random.

PRIYA
So the ledger was real. He never once mentioned it to me.

OKAFOR
People protect the ones they love from the parts that could hurt them. Looks like that's what got him killed.

EXT. KASPRZAK'S APARTMENT COMPLEX - DAY

Two unmarked cars flank the entrance. Priya watches from across the street as officers walk Dev out in cuffs, head down, not looking at anyone.

INT. OYELARAN HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY

Weeks later. Priya stands at the same counter, the same mixing bowl in front of her, folding batter the way she was taught instead of stirring it.

PRIYA
(to herself)
Fold it, don't stir it.

She almost smiles. It's the first time she has, in this room, since the night the window broke.

EXT. OYELARAN HOUSE - DAY

Priya steps out onto the porch where she once sat wrapped in a blanket, coffee in hand this time, and looks at the street like she's deciding whether it's still hers. She decides it is, and goes back inside.
